**Edit 2: body** — replace the gradient background with a simple fallback + add the sky layers:
```css
  body{
    font-family:...;
    color:var(--ink);
    line-height:1.7;
    background:var(--page-bg, #f2f9ff);
    min-height:100vh;
  }
```
Wait, I'm not defining a --page-bg variable; let's do it this way: day: `background:#eef6ff;` and night override `body.night{ background:#050d1f; }` — but the background is covered by the fixed layers anyway; still, the fallback is nice. Put `background:#eef6ff;` on body and add to the body.night variable block: `background:#050d1f;`. Add a background transition to body? The body's background-color transitions smoothly, but it's hidden behind the layers. Skip the transition on body.

Add the sky layer CSS:
```css
  /* ---------- Sky background layers (day/night crossfade) ---------- */
  .sky-day, .sky-night{ position:fixed; inset:0; z-index:-2; pointer-events:none; }
  .sky-day{
    z-index:-1;
    background:
      radial-gradient(1200px 500px at 80% -10%, #cfe8ff 0%, rgba(207,232,255,0) 60%),
      linear-gradient(180deg,#f2f9ff 0%, #ffffff 420px);
    transition:opacity .7s ease;
  }
  body.night .sky-day{ opacity:0; }
  .sky-night{
    background:
      linear-gradient(180deg,#030817 0%, #081533 55%, #12294f 100%);
  }
  .sky-night::after{ /* Milky Way band */
    content:""; position:absolute; inset:0;
    background:linear-gradient(115deg, transparent 32%, rgba(120,150,220,.08) 45%, rgba(170,190,235,.14) 50%, rgba(120,150,220,.08) 55%, transparent 68%);
  }
  .stars{ position:absolute; inset:0; background-repeat:repeat; }
  .stars.s1{
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 12px 44px, #fff, transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 66px 128px, rgba(255,255,255,.85), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 132px 30px, #fff, transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 182px 92px, rgba(255,255,255,.7), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 94px 182px, #fff, transparent 60%);
    background-size:220px 220px;
    animation:twinkle 3.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
  .stars.s2{
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 40px 20px, rgba(255,255,255,.9), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 150px 84px, #fff, transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 260px 140px, rgba(255,255,255,.75), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 90px 230px, #fff, transparent 60%);
    background-size:320px 320px;
    animation:twinkle 4.6s ease-in-out -1.4s infinite;
  }
  .stars.s3{
    background-image:
      radial-gradient(2px 2px at 70px 60px, #fff, transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(2px 2px at 230px 190px, rgba(255,255,255,.9), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(1px 1px at 320px 90px, rgba(190,215,255,.9), transparent 60%);
    background-size:480px 480px;
    animation:twinkle 5.8s ease-in-out -2.6s infinite;
  }
  @keyframes twinkle{ 0%,100%{ opacity:.5; } 50%{ opacity:1; } }
```

**Edit 3: Hero layers + sun/moon:**
```css
  .hero{
    position:relative;
    overflow:hidden;
    color:#fff;
    padding:64px 20px 72px;
    border-radius:0 0 28px 28px;
    box-shadow:var(--shadow);
  }
  .hero-sky{ position:absolute; inset:0; pointer-events:none; transition:opacity .7s ease; }
  .hero-day{
    z-index:0;
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0d2b52 0%, #1565c0 55%, #42a5f5 100%);
  }
  .hero-night{
    z-index:1;
    background:linear-gradient(135deg,#020617 0%, #0a1c44 55%, #17346b 100%);
  }
  body.night .hero-day{ opacity:0; }
```
Wait, the stacking: I want the night layer always present and the day layer on top of it, with the day layer fading out to reveal the night. So day z-index:2, night z-index:1, sun z-index:3, hero-inner z-index:4? Let's set it up like this:
- .hero-night { z-index:0 }
- .hero-day { z-index:1 }
- .sun { z-index:2 }
- .hero-inner { position:relative; z-index:3 }
And body.night .hero-day{opacity:0}. The hero-day is initially opacity:1, transition .7s.

Sun → moon:
```css
  .sun{
    position:absolute; top:-60px; right:8%;
    width:180px; height:180px; border-radius:50%;
    pointer-events:none; z-index:2;
  }
  .sun::before, .sun::after{
    content:""; position:absolute; inset:38px; border-radius:50%;
    transition:opacity .7s ease;
  }
  .sun::before{ /* daytime sun */
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, #fff7d6 0%, #ffe082 45%, rgba(255,224,130,0) 72%);
    filter:blur(2px);
  }
```
Hmm, the original had a blurred glow as the background on the whole .sun, plus a ::after core. Let me restructure:
- .sun (180px) contains two circles:
  - .sun-day-core (pseudo ::before): full 180px glow gradient, blur(2px) — day only.
  - .sun-moon (pseudo ::after): the core at inset 38px, with a pale color — night only.
But ::before has no inset → fills the whole 180px (glow). ::after is inset 38px (104px moon disk).

```css
  .sun::before{
    content:""; position:absolute; inset:0; border-radius:50%;
    background:radial-gradient(circle at 50% 50%, #fff7d6 0%, #ffe082 45%, rgba(255,224,130,0) 72%);
    filter:blur(2px);
    transition:opacity .7s ease;
  }
  .sun::after{
    content:""; position:absolute; inset:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:
      radial-gradient(circle at 62% 58%, rgba(96,112,145,.35) 0 7%, transparent 8%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 40% 68%, rgba(96,112,145,.3) 0 4.5%, transparent 5.5%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 56% 30%, rgba(96,112,145,.25) 0 3.5%, transparent 4.5%),
      radial-gradient(circle at 35% 35%, #f5f7fb 0%, #ccd6e6 60%, #b7c2d6 100%);
    opacity:0;
    transition:opacity .7s ease;
  }
  body.night .sun::before{ opacity:0; }
  body.night .sun::after{ opacity:1; }
```
This crossfades the sun's glow into a cratered moon.

