**Edit 7: Theme transition rule.** Add after the language-mode section:
```css
  /* ---------- Theme (day/night) transitions ---------- */
  .card, .card-head, .col, .col-label, .equation, .controls, .lang-toggle, .lang-btn,
  footer, footer code, #topBtn, .theme-btn, body{
    transition:background-color .6s ease, color .6s ease, border-color .6s ease, box-shadow .6s ease;
  }
```
Hmm, `body` — the body's color variable changes → text color transitions. Good. But note: `.lang-btn` already has its own transition (background .2s, color .2s, box-shadow .2s) — my rule with the same specificity (.lang-btn = 0,1,0) — the later rule wins. If I place the theme rule later in the stylesheet, it overrides lang-btn's .2s with .6s. That changes the feel of the button hover. Better to keep the lang-btn's own transition and not include it in the shared rule; the lang-btn's transition already covers background .2s color .2s box-shadow .2s — but when the theme changes, its background variable changes (transparent, no change) and the color variable changes — covered by its own transition. The active button's background variable change is also covered. OK: exclude .lang-btn from the shared rule, keep its own.

Also `.js .card` has a transition (opacity .6s, transform .6s) — it overrides the shared .card rule (higher specificity .js .card = 0,2,0). So when JS is on, the card's background-color transition is lost. Fix: update the `.js .card` rule to include the theme transitions:
```css
  .js .card{ opacity:0; transform:translateY(22px); transition:opacity .6s ease, transform .6s ease, background-color .6s ease, border-color .6s ease, box-shadow .6s ease; }
```
And keep the shared rule for non-JS.

`.card-head`, `.equation`, `.col-label`, `.controls` — the shared rule covers these (no conflicting own transitions). `.col` — no own transition. footer — none. #topBtn has its own: `transition:opacity .25s, transform .25s, background .2s;` — it overrides (same specificity? #topBtn = 1,0,0 ID → higher). So keep topBtn's own, and it already includes background .2s → covers the background change. Good, exclude #topBtn from the shared rule? The background transition is already there. Yes, exclude it.

`.theme-btn` — define its own transition in its rule.

body: the shared rule adds a color transition. Fine.

Also `.hero-inner`'s text (white) doesn't change. `.eyebrow` is fine.

One more: the `.controls`'s backdrop blur + background-color transition works (background-color is animatable).

**Edit 8: Theme button HTML + CSS.** Inside the controls nav, after the lang-toggle:
```html
    <div class="controls-right">
      <div class="lang-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Choose language">...</div>
      <button id="themeBtn" class="theme-btn" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
        <span class="theme-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="theme-label"><span class="t-en">Night Mode</span><span class="sep">·</span><span class="t-zh" lang="zh">夜晚模式</span></span>
      </button>
