```

**HTML structural edits:**
- After `<body class="mode-both">`, add:
```html
  <div class="sky-night" aria-hidden="true"><div class="stars s1"></div><div class="stars s2"></div><div class="stars s3"></div></div>
  <div class="sky-day" aria-hidden="true"></div>
```
Wait, z-index: .sky-night z-index:-2, .sky-day z-index:-1. Both behind the body content (body content has no z-index → is it auto-stacked above negative z-index? A fixed element with a negative z-index goes behind the body's background? The body's background is painted on the canvas... actually, a child with a negative z-index is painted behind the body's content but above the body's background (the background of the root stacking context). The body has a background → painted as the canvas background (html/body background propagates to the canvas). An element with z-index:-1 is painted above the canvas background and below other content. Yes, this works: the layers are above the body's background color and below all the content.

But note: the `.sky-night`'s child `.stars` — .sky-night is position:fixed, inset:0, z-index:-2 — its children are absolutely positioned within it (position:absolute, inset:0) — .sky-night needs position (it has fixed). OK. And the ::after for the Milky Way. The stars' opacity animation is fine.

One concern: `backdrop-filter` on .controls (z-index 20, fixed/sticky) blurs what's behind it → the stars show through the blurred controls bar. Nice effect.

